Buying a car is a big ticket purchase, and it’s incredibly frustrating when you end up with a lemon. The car stays in the shop, but your lender continues to deduct your car payments - it’s a situation nobody wants to face.

While there is no specific data for car dealerships in Orange County, [1.77 million](https://www.cncda.org/news/california-new-car-dealers-association-releases-q1-2024-auto-outlook-report) new cars and trucks were sold in California in 2023, which increases the likelihood of OC residents like you buying a lemon. Although you can file a claim, dealing with this situation is not easy, especially talking to the dealer who cheated you.

Handling your dealer is a necessary evil in lemon law disputes, and as leading [car dealer dispute lawyers in Orange County](/), we have seen these situations go from bad to worse. When dealing with a car dealer dispute, you must remain calm and avoid making mistakes that might overturn your claim.

Let’s find out how.

## What is a Lemon Law Dispute?

Lemon law deals with defective cars and other consumer goods bought under the warranty. A lemon law dispute arises when a new or used car buyer finds out that their car has substantial defects. When the dealer or manufacturer is unable to fix your car, despite several attempts, it can lead to a lemon law dispute. In California, [The Lemon Law](https://oag.ca.gov/consumers/general/cars) applies to new cars, used cars, and [leased vehicles](https://oag.ca.gov/consumers/general/cars).

## Tips for Dealing with Car Dealerships in Orange County

Handling car dealer disputes is a daunting task, but with the right approach, you can protect your rights as a consumer. Here are a few tips that can help:

## 1. Talk to Your Dealership

If you suspect your car might be a lemon, report the problem to your car dealership in writing. Many dealerships in Orange County are willing to work with their customers to resolve the issue immediately. Besides, the law requires you to give the dealer or manufacturer a reasonable number of attempts to fix your car.

When talking to the dealer, describe your problem in detail and provide copies of any documentation you have. Sometimes, the issue can be resolved quickly and without any legal action.

## 2. Don’t Feel Frustrated

Handling car dealership disputes can be frustrating, especially if your dealer tries to cheat you. Frustration often leads to hasty decisions that may not be in your best interests. Sometimes, you might feel like you have hit the wall when talking to your dealer but know that these are all negotiation tactics.

Some car dealerships in Orange County may quickly lawyer up to protect their interests. Your dealer might also resort to these legal tactics to trick you into accepting a lowball offer. However, you must continue to assert your rights and document all your interactions with the dealer.

## 3. Know Your Rights

Sometimes, the dealer might try to avoid their responsibility, but knowing your rights can prevent you from falling for these tactics. According to California’s Lemon Law, your vehicle meets the criteria of a lemon, if:

- It has a substantial defect covered by the warranty.

- The defect persists after a reasonable number of repair attempts.

- The vehicle has been out of service for repairs for a significant number of days.

You can check out our [California Lemon Law](/info/a-comprehensive-guide-to-california-lemon-law) guide for more information.

## 4. Document Everything

Resolving car dealer disputes involves a lot of back and forth, and you must document everything from day one. Report your car trouble in writing, asking the dealership to fix the problem under warranty. Keep detailed records of all interactions with the dealer, including:

- Repair attempts

- Repair related documents and bills

- Written communication, such as letters or reports

- Phone calls, emails, and in-person meetings

Note the dates, times, names of individuals you spoke with, and what was discussed or agreed upon. This documentation can be critical if your dispute escalates.

## 5. Don’t Sign or Accept Anything

We have said this before and will say it again - DON’T ACCEPT any offer or sign a piece of paper from your dealer. Sometimes, the dealership may offer a settlement that is far below what you lawfully deserve. Avoid [negotiating a settlement](/blog/tips-for-negotiating-with-car-dealers-in-a-lemon-case) yourself, instead talk to a car dealer dispute lawyer immediately to know more about your lemon law claim.

## 6. Talk to a Car Dealer Dispute Lawyer

Consult a skilled car dealer dispute lawyer as soon as possible. A lawyer specializing in lemon law can help you understand your rights. They can study your case and explain your legal options.

Lawyers are expert negotiators. They can represent you in negotiations and help you land a favorable settlement. And although it’s less likely to happen, your lawyer can represent you in court if your case goes to trial.

## Settle Car Dealership Dispute

Handling a lemon law dispute can be challenging, but it doesn’t have to be. When dealing with a lemon, stay calm and document all your interactions with the dealer. Avoid negotiating with the dealer alone, hire a lawyer specializing in car dealership disputes as soon as possible. Taking these steps might help you get a fair solution.

